    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d779">

                <pb facs="0844" n="746" xml:id="pg746"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.119/333: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-12T23:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 12,
                                1920—11 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 11:50
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>180. Your 169, July 10, 3 p.m. Naval attaché’s report of arms shipment is without
                    doubt correct. Tuan’s<anchor n="96" xml:id="d779-f1"/> troops have received
                    these arms. Complaint that Japanese also have shipped arms has been made
                    formally to diplomatic corps by Tsao Kun.<anchor n="97" xml:id="d779-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d779-f1" n="96" xml:id="d779-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Tuan Chih-kuei, commander in chief of the Anfu army.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d779-f2" n="97" xml:id="d779-f2-content">
                <p>Inspector general of Chihli, Shantung, and Honan Provinces.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
